WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.000
When we were kids, growing up in West Texas, our winters would be cold but rarely experienced snow. But we did have ice, which resulted in the roads being salted.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:22.000
As the salt mixes in and dissolves into water on the road, this can lead to a lower freezing point which can help prevent the roads from icing over.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.000
And while this is great for making the roads more safe, it wasn’t so great for the plants that lived right along the roadside. It often caused them to die.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Now winter can be hard for many plant species, but I’m talking about this salt affecting even some hardy plant life.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.000
This issue with salt and plants isn’t limited to winter.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:47.000
During hurricanes near the coast, salty ocean water can be dumped in large quantities into soil.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:54.000
While the effect may not be instant, this can eventually actually kill plants, including trees, that had originally survived the hurricane. Why?

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Do plants just dislike salt that much? Well, it’s actually related to this awesome term: osmosis.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.000
When you are talking about osmosis, you are talking about the movement of water through a semi-permeable membrane, like a cell membrane.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Water molecules are so small that they can travel through the cell membrane unassisted, or they can travel in larger quantities through protein channels like aquaporins.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.000
The movement of water molecules traveling across a cell membrane is passive transport, which means it does not require energy.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:33.000
In osmosis, water molecules travel from areas of a high concentration (of water molecules) to a low concentration (of water molecules).

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:42.000
But there’s another way to think about water movement in osmosis. A low water concentration likely means there is a greater solute concentration.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Solutes are substances, like salt or sugar, that can be dissolved within a solvent, like water.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Water has the tendency to move to areas where there is a higher solute concentration, which would mean less water concentration.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:07.000
So if you want to easily figure out where the water will travel, look to the side where there is a greater solute concentration.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Unless we bring in another variable, like pressure, water will generally have a net movement to the area of higher solute concentration.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:21.000
So let’s bring out a U-tube! Ha, U-tube. That’s funny. There’s a semi-permeable membrane in the middle of it.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:29.000
Let’s assume that it is similar to a cell membrane in that water molecules can squeeze through it (the molecules are quite small) but salt can’t.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Right now, there is just water in this U-tube. The water levels on side A and side B are equal.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:43.000
That doesn’t mean that the water molecules aren’t moving (water molecules like to move) but the net movement across the two sides is zero.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:52.000
That means the overall change in the direction of movement is zero. Now let’s imagine on side B, you dump a huge amount of salt there.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:03:01.000
So which direction will the water initially move towards, A or B? Think about what we mentioned with osmosis.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.000
The answer is B!

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Side B has a higher solute concentration than side A. Water moves to areas of higher solute concentration, which is also the area of lower water concentration.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.000
The water level on side B will be higher in the U-tube.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:35.000
You can almost think of the water as trying to equalize the concentrations, diluting side B. Once equilibrium is reached, the net movement of water across the two sides will be zero, but remember that water still likes to move and movement still occurs.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Here’s some vocabulary to add in here. We call side B hypertonic. This means higher solute concentration!

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.000
But we can’t just say something is hypertonic without comparing it to something else.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:04:00.000
We say side B is hypertonic to side A because it has a higher solute concentration than side A. In osmosis, water moves to the hypertonic side.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
We say side A is hypotonic (hypo rhymes with low, which helps me remember low solute concentration)

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:18.000
when compared to side B. Let’s get a little more real life now instead of just the U-tube. As you know, water is important for your body and many processes that occur in the body.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:26.000
When someone gets an IV in a hospital, it may look like the fluid in the IV is just pure water. But it is certainly not pure water.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:32.000
That would be a disaster because of osmosis. Let’s explain. Let’s say hypothetically pure water was in an IV.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Now an IV tube typically runs through a vein, so that you have access to your bloodstream. Really useful for running medication through.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:49.000
Blood actually consists of many different types of components, and red blood cells are a great example. So what do you think has a higher solute concentration?

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:58.000
The hypothetical pure water in this IV tube? Or the red blood cells? Well, cells are not empty vessels. They contain solutes.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.000
The pure water that hypothetically is running through this IV tube has no solutes. So where does the water go?

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:11.000
It goes to the areas of higher solute concentration: inside the cells.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:18.000
The cells are hypertonic compared to the pure water in the IV tube because the cells have a greater solute concentration.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.000
The cells would swell and possibly burst! Exploding red blood cells are not good.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:29.000
If a person needs fluids, they typically will receive a solution that is isotonic to their blood plasma.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:37.000
Isotonic means equal concentration, so you won’t have any swelling or shrinking red blood cells. Or let’s talk about the aquarium.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:44.000
I have always wanted a saltwater fish tank, ever since I was a little kid. But I’ve only had freshwater tanks. So far.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:49.000
I did often question when I was a kid, why is it that a saltwater fish can’t be in my freshwater tank?

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:59.000
Well, let me explain one reason why this would be dangerous to a saltwater fish and how it relates to osmosis. First ask: where is there a higher solute concentration?

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:07.000
In the saltwater fish cells? Or in the freshwater that the fish would be placed in? Definitely in the saltwater fish cells.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:17.000
So where would the water go? It goes to the area where there is a higher solute concentration, the hypertonic side, so it goes into the cells of that poor saltwater fish.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:25.000
If not rescued, it could die. Now one thing to clarify: saltwater fish and freshwater fish are not necessarily isotonic to their surroundings.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:35.000
But they have special adaptations that allow them to live in their environment and usually cannot make a major switch from a saltwater environment to freshwater.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.000
Now, not all fish have this problem.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:45.000
There are some fish that have amazing adaptations to switch between fresh and salt water, and they have to deal with this osmosis problem.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:54.000
Salmon, for example. I think if I could pick to be a fish, I’d be a salmon. No question. Osmosis explains how many kinds of plants get their water.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:01.000
Sure, many plants have roots. But how does the water get in the roots? When it rains, the soil becomes saturated with water.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:09.000
The root hair cells generally have a higher concentration of solutes within them than the solute concentration in the saturated soil.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:15.000
The water travels into the root cells as the root hair cells are hypertonic compared to the hypotonic soil.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:15.900
By the way, you may wonder: well, why don’t those root hair cells burst with all that water?
